// ============================================================
// Shared functions (L6) — business rules that outlive one query
// ============================================================
// A rule written once and called from several places is the whole point of having
// functions in a model: the alternative is the same predicate copy-pasted into a dozen
// services, drifting apart. These are called extension-style, `$x->pkg::fn()`.
//
// They are also a distinct COMPILATION path — a function body is type-checked
// independently of any service, and its return multiplicity is part of its signature.
###Pure

// A rule that answers a question about a trade using two of its columns. Both are [1],
// so the arithmetic needs no optionality discharge — unlike netCost, which is why that
// one had to be withdrawn (F7).
function stress::rules::isLargeTrade(t: trading::Trade[1]): Boolean[1]
{
   ($t.quantity * $t.price) > 250000.0
}

// A rule over an ENUM-typed property. Reading an enum inside a function body is a
// different path from projecting it, and the corpus has three findings about enums being
// handled per-path (F10, F12, F13) — so it is worth pinning that this one agrees.
function stress::rules::isBuy(t: trading::Trade[1]): Boolean[1]
{
   $t.side == trading::Side.BUY
}

// A rule with a PARAMETER, so the same function answers differently per call.
function stress::rules::exceeds(t: trading::Trade[1], threshold: Float[1]): Boolean[1]
{
   ($t.quantity * $t.price) > $threshold
}

// A rule reached across an association: the function itself navigates.
function stress::rules::counterpartyRegion(t: trading::Trade[1]): String[0..1]
{
   $t.counterparty.country.region
}
